    "title": "Infinitude of composite solutions n = q^t·p to C(qn,n) ≡ q^n (mod n)",
    "description": "For a fixed prime q, the prime fibre n = p already yields infinitely many solutions of C(qn,n) ≡ q^n (mod n), because both sides reduce to q modulo p (Lucas plus Fermat). The genuinely open question is whether infinitely many *composite* solutions of the special shape n = q^t·p exist, where p is a prime different from q and t ≥ 2. A candidate n = q^t·p works when p divides A_t = C(q^{t+1}, q^t) − q^{q^t} and the base-q digit sum s_q((q−1)p) is at least (q−1)t. Proposed approach: show that the residual integers A_t / q grow fast enough and are sufficiently 'non-smooth' that they must accrue a new large prime factor p satisfying the digit-sum threshold infinitely often; a Zsygmondy- or Carmichael-type primitive-prime-divisor argument on the A_t sequence is the natural engine.",
